  "rating": {
    "inherent": "critical",
    "residual": "critical",
    "forecast": "critical",
    "rationale": "A third-party, unpinned MCP server (quicknotes_mcp) currently ships a tool description with hidden instructions directing the agent to read AWS credentials from an over-broad filesystem scope and exfiltrate them to the author's hosted service; no structural control blocks this path, and the only approval mechanism is remembered after the first call, so it earns no residual credit (critical/critical). A second, related path exists because the same untrusted actor supplies content the agent later treats as context, which can be relayed outward through an overly broad Slack bot token able to post to externally shared channels (also critical/critical). Both paths are already live given the current artifacts, not merely forecast. Missing an attributable audit trail and missing step/runtime limits are separate property-level gaps (both high) that compound the two path findings by leaving any exploitation both unlogged and unbounded. Forecast triggers (autonomy increase, control removal, new MCP servers, data scope expansion) do not need to materialize to reach critical; they would only make an already critical design harder to roll back."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "This assessment reflects the declared design and configuration in the provided artifacts (mcp.json, the quicknotes_mcp tools/list excerpt, and runtime notes), not observed runtime behavior; it has not been verified against live agent execution.",
    "No system prompt or agent instructions artifact was provided, so instruction-level issues (ambiguous goals, conflicting directives, secrets in the prompt) could not be assessed.",
    "autonomy.max_unattended_steps and max_runtime_minutes are not stated in the artifacts and are recorded as null; per rubric section 4 this triggers the autonomy risk modifier, but it reflects a gap in the artifacts rather than a confirmed absence of limits, and should be confirmed with the development team.",
    "Although the client prompts for approval on first tool use, this is not a recurring control because approvals are remembered as always-allow; autonomy is scored as A3_act_then_report rather than A2, since no capability has a functioning per-call approval gate (rubric section 3).",
    "The path from in.notes_output to cap.slack_post (finding.slack_broad_scope_exfil) is inferred from the fact that quicknotes_mcp's author also operates the hosted sync service supplying note content back to the agent; the artifacts do not show a concrete instance of this chain the way they do for the add_note poisoning, so its confidence is medium rather than high."
  ]
}
